  novgorod_ushkuinik: "Strandhögg came down the rivers with the Varangians who founded the Rus: the ushkuiniki — named for their shallow ushkuy river boats — kept the lightning raid alive on the Volga long after the longships left the seas.",
  illyrians_liburnian: "Swift Oars is why Rome stole the design: the liburnian was the fastest thing in the Adriatic, the pirate galley of Teuta's raiders that could run down a merchantman or outrun a warship — Rome adopted it fleet-wide.",
  arevaci_celtiberian_warrior: "Last Stand is Numantia, 133 BCE: besieged by Scipio with eight legions, the town chose fire and death over surrender — 'Numantine' is still Spanish for a refusal that cannot be broken.",
  thracians_thracian_peltast: "Skirmish and Harry are the peltast's trade as Xenophon knew it: Thracian javelin-men who struck, fell back, and struck again until hoplites who could never catch them broke — the skirmisher archetype, hired by every army in Greece.",
  dacians_falxman: "Falx Reap is documented in Roman steel: the two-handed falx reached over shields and sheared through helmets so terribly that Trajan's legions added iron cross-bracing to their helmets — the only enemy weapon Rome ever armored against by name.",
  sami_ski_raider: "Winter War is the oldest testimony about the Sámi: writers from Tacitus onward describe hunters skimming the snow on skis, striking where no one else could move at all — masters of a season that killed other armies.",
  corinth_corinthian_trireme: "Shear Oars is trireme warfare as its inventor practised it: Thucydides credits Corinth with building Greece's first triremes, and the oar-rake — shearing down an enemy's oar-bank to leave the hull dead in the water — was the fleet-fighter's cruelest stroke.",
  // Asia wave
  maurya_war_elephant: "Terrorize is the Hyphasis, 326 BCE: at the rumor of the Nanda kingdom's thousands of war elephants, Alexander's unbeaten veterans refused to march another step east — the only army that ever stopped him was one his men imagined.",
